  "leaderboardHourUnit": "h",
  "@leaderboardRetry": {
    "description": "The failure arm's retry label. The board has its OWN key, and not `/reading`'s: `homeRetry`, `quizRetry`, `readingRetry` and `settingsRetry` are four separate keys with identical values, and the repository namespaces `retry` per screen on purpose. A fifth screen borrowing the fourth's key is the coupling this key removes. `eva/redesign.html:627`'s screen has no retry of its own; the `ErrorView` it borrows is the design system's."
  },
  "@leaderboardScopeLabel": {
    "description": "The ACCESSIBLE NAME of the scope segmented control — the week/month/all picker. It is a control's own name, and it must not be one of its own option labels: `SegmentedControl` puts this string on the track's node, and two activatable nodes with the same name is the failure `segmented_control.dart`'s own doc names. `eva/redesign.html:940`'s `week`/`month`/`all` row supplies the OPTIONS; this supplies the name of the thing that holds them."
  },
  "@leaderboardMetricLabel": {
    "description": "The ACCESSIBLE NAME of the metric segmented control — the light/streaks picker. Same rule as `leaderboardScopeLabel`: a control's name, never one of its own options. `eva/redesign.html:941`'s `light`/`streakM` row supplies the options; this supplies the name of the thing that holds them."
  },
  "@leaderboardDayUnit": {
    "description": "The day unit in the reset line — `d` in English, `ي` in Arabic. The prototype's own `dU` row (`eva/redesign.html:946`), and it is a KEY rather than a fork in the widget so that a third locale has somewhere to put its own letter instead of silently getting an English one. The Arabic value is `ي`, the prototype's own."
  },
  "@leaderboardHourUnit": {
    "description": "The hour unit in the reset line — `h` in English, `س` in Arabic. The prototype's own `hU` row (`eva/redesign.html:946`), and the same reasoning as `leaderboardDayUnit`."
  },
  "@leaderboardVeil": {
    "description": "The board header's eye button, and the Settings row Task 5.7 will build: replace every other witness's name with `witness {position}` and their monogram with the hollow star. `eva/redesign.html:948` `veilT`; applied at `:1069-1072`.\n\nA rendering choice over rows that already arrived — there is no veil endpoint (ADR-0022 `:243-245`)."
  },
  "@leaderboardRowSemantics": {
    "description": "The screen-reader sentence for one ranked row.\n\nA REAL ICU PLURAL, and the reason this key exists rather than four separate strings. A row is four numbers and a monogram; a screen reader announcing \"3, PG, 80, points\" gives a reader four fragments and no sentence, so this composes one. The plural is over `count` with the digits interpolated separately, which is the same shape `readingCaptionFor` uses.\n\n`isReader` is a `select`, not a second plural: \"that is you\" is a clause, not a quantity, and folding it into the plural would multiply the arms for no gain.\n\nNEVER A NAME. `initials` is the monogram and nothing else can reach this string, which is the accessibility half of `LeaderboardRow` having no `fullName` — a voice-over user must not be able to hear a name the screen cannot show.\n\n`rank` and `digits` are **String** while `count` is **int**, and that split is load-bearing rather than fussy: `count` is what the plural selects on, and `digits` is what the reader sees. Passing the numeral twice means the shape does not have to change when a locale's digits are not ASCII — the Arabic arm renders U+0660…U+0669, and `intl`'s formatting would otherwise be a second source of truth about numerals. `readingCaptionFor` does the same thing.",
    "placeholders": {
      "count": {
        "type": "int",
        "example": "80"
      },
      "digits": {
        "type": "String",
        "example": "80"
      },
      "streakCount": {
        "type": "int",
        "example": "12"
      },
      "streakDigits": {
        "type": "String",
        "example": "12"
      },
      "initials": {
        "type": "String",
        "example": "PG"
      },
      "rank": {
        "type": "String",
        "example": "3"
      },
      "isReader": {
        "type": "String",
        "example": "true"
      }
    }
  },
  "@leaderboardEmptyTitle": {
    "description": "Shown when the cohort has nobody ranked.\n\nA REAL STATE, NOT AN ERROR. Measured against the live endpoint: `{\"group_id\":3,\"window\":\"weekly\",\"total_ranked\":0,\"my_standing\":null,\"leaderboard\":[]}`. Scores only enter Redis via `leaderboardService.recordScore`, called from `submissions.service.ts:105` on a *successful* submission, and submission cannot succeed against the memory fallback because the schemas require UUIDs the fallback does not mint. So the empty board is currently the ONLY reachable state in development.\n\nThis is why the copy explains what will fill it instead of apologising for a failure."
  },
  "@leaderboardEmptyBody": {
    "description": "Body under `leaderboardEmptyTitle`. Says what will populate the list, because the reader cannot tell whether the cohort is new or the app is broken."
  },
  "@leaderboardUnranked": {
    "description": "Shown instead of a position when the client cannot place the reader.\n\n**TWO SITUATIONS, and they are not the same.** `LeaderboardStanding.rank` is `null` — the server has no ranking for this reader at all. And `rank` is not `null` but the reader's **row is off the page** (the endpoint's `limit` is 50), so under the `streaks` metric — which the server does not rank and the client cannot rank a reader it has no row for — the card says the reader is unranked **on this board**.\n\nNEVER AN ARITHMETIC RESULT. `rank: null` with `score: 0` is the server's measured answer for a child who has not scored. Rendering it as `0` or `1` would put a fabricated position on someone who has none — the defect `LeaderboardStanding`'s doc is named for."
  },
